1
00:00:12,439 --> 00:00:14,340
[SPEAKER_02]: The simile of the six animals.

2
00:00:17,222 --> 00:00:22,125
[SPEAKER_02]: Suppose, monks, a man catches six animals of different domains and different resorts.

3
00:00:22,885 --> 00:00:30,530
[SPEAKER_02]: A snake, a crocodile, a bird, dog, a jackal and a monkey, tethering each with a strong rope.

4
00:00:31,911 --> 00:00:35,934
[SPEAKER_02]: Having tethered them with a strong rope, he fastens the rope together in the middle.

5
00:00:35,994 --> 00:00:36,694
[SPEAKER_02]: He lets go of them.

6
00:00:38,205 --> 00:00:46,571
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, monks, these six animals of different domains and feeding habits would swing around and struggle, each trying to get to the... to his natural domain.

7
00:00:47,792 --> 00:00:51,315
[SPEAKER_02]: The snake would struggle, the crocodile and so on.

8
00:00:54,658 --> 00:01:03,785
[SPEAKER_02]: Now, monks, when those six hungry animals grow weary, they would yield to the one that was the strongest, go his way and be under his power.

9
00:01:04,807 --> 00:01:17,540
[SPEAKER_02]: In the same way, monks, whenever a monk fails to practice and develop mindfulness as to the body, the eye struggles to draw him towards attractive objects, while unattractive objects are repellent to him.

10
00:01:19,262 --> 00:01:23,846
[SPEAKER_02]: And so, this monk is lack of restraint.

11
00:01:24,807 --> 00:01:26,329
[SPEAKER_02]: And what monks is restraint?

12
00:01:27,908 --> 00:01:38,072
[SPEAKER_02]: So, instead of... he ties all those animals to a strong post, and then they all pull in their different directions, grow tired, and stay right there.

13
00:01:38,312 --> 00:01:40,953
[SPEAKER_02]: And that post is mindfulness of the body.

14
00:01:44,955 --> 00:01:55,159
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the... would you... can you have... the strain of the six senses, so including your mind,

15
00:01:57,457 --> 00:02:12,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Without the... without the right context, without... like, if you don't have perspective, in regard to the... all of the six senses, so, without a perspective, can you restrain them?

16
00:02:16,033 --> 00:02:17,254
[SPEAKER_01]: No, because you're already pulled.

17
00:02:17,394 --> 00:02:23,838
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why non-perspective, lack of perspective, means you're down the the pull of a sense or all the senses.

18
00:02:24,619 --> 00:02:33,966
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why people don't have... like people who engage with sensuality, they would have called sensuality weakeners of wisdom, weakeners of perspective, weakeners of things that you know.

19
00:02:34,882 --> 00:02:40,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you get pulled down the... down the... the pathway of a sense, of that wild animal.

20
00:02:41,403 --> 00:02:44,964
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you cannot sustain perspective and at the same time go down the rabbit hole.

21
00:02:45,084 --> 00:02:45,904
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

22
00:02:46,004 --> 00:02:49,945
[SPEAKER_01]: You either allow and go down the rabbit hole or you sustain the perspective.

23
00:02:50,465 --> 00:02:57,987
[SPEAKER_01]: The reason why I'm bringing perspective... the perspective up is because people would often think that mindfulness of the body means, like, feeling the body here.

24
00:02:58,587 --> 00:03:01,609
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, feeling the sensations of it means I'm mindful of this body.

25
00:03:02,230 --> 00:03:02,370
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

26
00:03:02,390 --> 00:03:09,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness of the body is the fundamental perspective in regard to your own world, as in the presence of the body there, as Satipaṭṭhāna Sutta says.

27
00:03:10,716 --> 00:03:16,520
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the body there is the basis for these six animals, to which they are tied to, whether they want to or not.

28
00:03:16,981 --> 00:03:21,004
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't have sense organ without the body, without a unit of the body.

29
00:03:22,165 --> 00:03:26,428
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why mindfulness of the body would mean mindfulness of the perspective in regard to all the six.

30
00:03:28,177 --> 00:03:29,037
[SPEAKER_02]: Senses that you have.

31
00:03:31,558 --> 00:03:37,861
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not a physical way of, like, method, practice of staying in the body.

32
00:03:37,901 --> 00:03:44,624
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on the level of knowledge of the body there, its nature, its post, from which these senses want to pull left and right.

33
00:03:45,764 --> 00:03:53,548
[SPEAKER_01]: But yes, if you contemplate the nature of that body, as the Buddha would say, as something that is unownable, subject to illness, death and so on,

34
00:03:54,477 --> 00:04:07,265
[SPEAKER_01]: No pleasure of the senses within the container of the body would be able to overwhelm that perspective, if it's thoroughly developed.

35
00:04:08,686 --> 00:04:13,749
[SPEAKER_02]: But if you see your body as not mine, that's the right perspective.

36
00:04:13,769 --> 00:04:20,233
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, if you see the body on the right level as not mine, not just, oh yes, this body is not mine,

37
00:04:21,620 --> 00:04:33,625
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you think you're seeing the body on the right level, as not mine, you see, while I'm seeing the body on the right level, as not mine, is there passion and liability to passion in this mind?

38
00:04:34,205 --> 00:04:37,066
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there aversion and liability to aversion in this mind?

39
00:04:37,146 --> 00:04:39,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there distraction and liability to distraction in this mind?

40
00:04:39,968 --> 00:04:43,349
[SPEAKER_01]: If the answer to any of those is yes, that's not the right level.

41
00:04:44,509 --> 00:04:48,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Because seeing the body on the right level, it is impenetrable.

42
00:04:49,382 --> 00:05:02,308
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you, with the mind not above passion, aversion, delusion and liability to passion, aversion, delusion, thinks this is the right level of the mindfulness of the body, well, you're confusing yourself.

43
00:05:03,289 --> 00:05:12,893
[SPEAKER_01]: That is a serious misconception, because of which you will then not make the effort to find what the right level is, the level that is not liable to greed, aversion and delusion.

44
00:05:21,121 --> 00:05:21,721
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in what way?

45
00:05:21,961 --> 00:05:24,282
[SPEAKER_02]: How do we be mindful of the body?

46
00:05:26,382 --> 00:05:30,783
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, through understanding its... Well, one way would be through understanding its nature.

47
00:05:31,264 --> 00:05:37,225
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just through, like... Because, as I said, be mindful of the body and you have some idea about it and how you're doing it.

48
00:05:37,705 --> 00:05:43,527
[SPEAKER_01]: But instead of focusing on doing the mindfulness of the body, focus on understanding the idea of what you think mindfulness of the body is.

49
00:05:44,827 --> 00:05:46,507
[SPEAKER_01]: Because people just take that idea for granted.

50
00:05:46,527 --> 00:05:48,528
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I hear mindfulness of the body

51
00:05:49,463 --> 00:05:54,667
[SPEAKER_01]: As an instruction, and whatever comes to my mind that I do, is mindfulness of the body.

52
00:05:54,708 --> 00:05:56,189
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, isn't that fortunate?

53
00:05:57,590 --> 00:05:58,791
[SPEAKER_01]: If it were so.

54
00:05:59,472 --> 00:06:07,959
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you know, it's the idea, that's where the context, the right context, the wrong context, is basically right idea, wrong idea, or right ideas, wrong ideas.

55
00:06:08,699 --> 00:06:12,783
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, you're gonna have some notion of the mindfulness of the body and some notion of the sense restraint.

56
00:06:13,532 --> 00:06:16,273
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have to do it, in a sense of you have to be sensory-strained.

57
00:06:16,733 --> 00:06:25,995
[SPEAKER_01]: But instead of taking the notion for granted, through which you just keep doing this technique of mindfulness of the body or whatever it is for you, you actually keep dwelling on the notion and clarify it further.

58
00:06:26,976 --> 00:06:28,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Does it correspond to what the suttas describe?

59
00:06:29,356 --> 00:06:31,457
[SPEAKER_01]: Does it make you dispassionate completely?

60
00:06:32,057 --> 00:06:36,178
[SPEAKER_01]: Even when you are not momentarily focused on the body.

61
00:06:37,773 --> 00:06:44,937
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it an anchor that allows you to then move around dispassionate, not liable to anger, not liable to delusion?

62
00:06:45,357 --> 00:06:49,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Or is it something that you're dispassionate and not angry and so on, only when you're focusing on the body?

63
00:06:49,900 --> 00:06:51,861
[SPEAKER_01]: In which case, that's not mindfulness of the body.

64
00:06:51,881 --> 00:06:53,922
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just you replacing one object with the other object.

65
00:06:54,082 --> 00:06:56,944
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you've got to have some idea.

66
00:06:56,984 --> 00:06:58,144
[SPEAKER_02]: You will have some idea.

67
00:06:58,705 --> 00:07:01,506
[SPEAKER_02]: And then... but you can't forget about that idea.

68
00:07:01,566 --> 00:07:02,467
[SPEAKER_02]: You have to practice it.

69
00:07:02,507 --> 00:07:02,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

70
00:07:03,267 --> 00:07:06,189
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to be upgrading the idea behind

71
00:07:07,180 --> 00:07:09,101
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you want to be developing understanding.

72
00:07:09,621 --> 00:07:15,264
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just whatever you assumed mindfulness of body is, is what it is, and now you're just doing it.

73
00:07:17,245 --> 00:07:23,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if that mindfulness of the body were the correct mindfulness of the body, you would be at least anāgāmī.

74
00:07:25,049 --> 00:07:26,910
[SPEAKER_01]: You would have no more central pull.

75
00:07:29,592 --> 00:07:35,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when the mindfulness of the body is understood to the extent necessary, you're enlightened.

76
00:07:39,766 --> 00:07:54,910
[SPEAKER_01]: So, people conflate understanding, like, fulfilling the understanding of mindfulness of the body in this case, with doing enough of mindfulness of the body that I just assumed mindfulness to be.

77
00:07:57,251 --> 00:08:04,493
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's like, okay, maybe you were lucky, maybe your assumption of the mindfulness of the body was really correct right from the start, but what's the chance of that today?

78
00:08:05,855 --> 00:08:13,359
[SPEAKER_01]: And also, how many years have you been doing it, and are there the results to show for, even when you're not mindful of the body with your effort?

79
00:08:17,140 --> 00:08:21,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's why people would also like fall into the view that, oh yeah, this is the dispassion.

80
00:08:21,803 --> 00:08:30,547
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because, yeah, there is no passion, and ill-will, and current delusion, when you're actively making this effort to be focused on body or something.

81
00:08:31,785 --> 00:08:37,888
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that object in itself of your focusing, yeah, it's not arising in passion and so on, but are you liable?

82
00:08:38,169 --> 00:08:40,470
[SPEAKER_01]: When you stop the focusing, will the passion come back?

83
00:08:41,070 --> 00:08:47,154
[SPEAKER_01]: Means there is no understanding that has taken root, which would have stayed, regardless of whether you're focusing on the body or not.

84
00:08:48,714 --> 00:08:50,355
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's same with the sensory strains.

85
00:08:50,636 --> 00:08:52,877
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the five animals, it's not just

86
00:08:54,347 --> 00:08:59,190
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the problem, it's not that the five animals want to go and feed in these different domains.

87
00:09:00,351 --> 00:09:03,994
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is that you don't know how to not identify with them.

88
00:09:04,714 --> 00:09:07,156
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you don't know how to not take them as mine.

89
00:09:07,976 --> 00:09:15,902
[SPEAKER_01]: When I want to see something, ears want to hear something, your first starting point is, I want to see something, I want to hear something.

90
00:09:16,542 --> 00:09:19,404
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you hammer down the restraint on yourself.

91
00:09:19,864 --> 00:09:21,305
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where restraint is unpleasant.

92
00:09:21,906 --> 00:09:23,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is an inherent discrepancy there.

93
00:09:26,137 --> 00:09:28,998
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I say, you shouldn't be trying to get rid of these things.

94
00:09:29,958 --> 00:09:34,980
[SPEAKER_01]: You should restrain yourself, but you can only do that correctly on the basis of allowing these things to endure.

95
00:09:35,060 --> 00:09:37,401
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your eyes want to see something.

96
00:09:37,821 --> 00:09:42,523
[SPEAKER_01]: You restrain eyes from seeing, but you don't ignore the fact that eyes want to see something.

97
00:09:42,763 --> 00:09:43,543
[SPEAKER_01]: That's unpleasant.

98
00:09:44,243 --> 00:09:51,646
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you get to, basically, outline the domain of the eye, that you realize, ah, okay, this is what an eye does.

99
00:09:52,988 --> 00:09:54,309
[SPEAKER_02]: You don't want to strangle the eye.

100
00:09:54,449 --> 00:09:55,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

101
00:09:55,970 --> 00:09:59,672
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will be strangling the eye, so to speak, because you are identified with it.

102
00:09:59,892 --> 00:10:00,852
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's self-destruct.

103
00:10:00,872 --> 00:10:02,353
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the only way out that you can see.

104
00:10:03,354 --> 00:10:09,437
[SPEAKER_01]: But even the simile says it's these six animals that they are tied to and they will pull everybody else.

105
00:10:09,737 --> 00:10:15,220
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are the one that should be in charge of the six animals, not be pulled by them.

106
00:10:15,988 --> 00:10:22,832
[SPEAKER_01]: But the reason you're pulled by them is because you cannot cease to identify with it.

107
00:10:23,033 --> 00:10:27,355
[SPEAKER_01]: When the pull is there, first, you feel like you're being pulled in that direction.

108
00:10:28,776 --> 00:10:30,017
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you have to start.

109
00:10:31,598 --> 00:10:42,305
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you start by being pulled, you start by not giving in to the pull, but if all you do is just try to get rid of the pull, you're basically trying to remove the senses, trying to kill the animals.

110
00:10:42,745 --> 00:10:44,206
[SPEAKER_01]: That still doesn't make you in charge.

111
00:10:44,426 --> 00:10:45,527
[SPEAKER_01]: That just kills the animals.

112
00:10:47,190 --> 00:10:52,013
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if somebody says, yeah, I would not see sights with my eyes, that's how I'm free.

113
00:10:52,313 --> 00:10:53,853
[SPEAKER_01]: I would not hear sounds with my ears.

114
00:10:54,074 --> 00:10:56,375
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said, well, you're no different than a blind and a deaf man.

115
00:10:56,915 --> 00:10:57,855
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not free at all.

116
00:10:58,376 --> 00:11:00,376
[SPEAKER_01]: You just have one animal left pulling you around.

117
00:11:00,396 --> 00:11:01,437
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't need six.

118
00:11:01,597 --> 00:11:03,058
[SPEAKER_01]: It's enough for one of them to pull you around.

119
00:11:03,418 --> 00:11:06,359
[SPEAKER_01]: And for as long as you're alive, you will at least have one, at least your mind.

120
00:11:06,419 --> 00:11:11,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if all of your senses fail, you're kind of locked inside syndrome or something, you have your mind.

121
00:11:11,802 --> 00:11:13,162
[SPEAKER_01]: And that mind will be pulling you now

122
00:11:16,232 --> 00:11:20,353
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the point is to you not be pulled through identification with that sense.

123
00:11:20,894 --> 00:11:22,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Not to not have the sense pulling.

124
00:11:25,555 --> 00:11:27,176
[SPEAKER_02]: Through non-identification.

125
00:11:27,256 --> 00:11:27,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

126
00:11:27,836 --> 00:11:32,037
[SPEAKER_01]: And when I say non-identification, again, it should not be understood like as a matter of choice.

127
00:11:32,117 --> 00:11:33,518
[SPEAKER_01]: I choose to identify.

128
00:11:34,358 --> 00:11:34,478
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

129
00:11:34,518 --> 00:11:42,341
[SPEAKER_01]: By not being able to resist the pull, by not having the clear context and perspective in regard to the senses, you are automatically identified.

130
00:11:43,181 --> 00:11:45,422
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't just choose to say this is not mine.

131
00:11:46,607 --> 00:11:50,369
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can set yourself up so that you're unable to take it as mine.

132
00:11:52,110 --> 00:11:57,893
[SPEAKER_01]: And you set yourself up by actually sustaining the perspective in regard to the various pulling senses.

133
00:11:59,434 --> 00:12:11,020
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you're contrasting now these senses against the better, more wholesome, broader perspective that corresponds closer to the actual universal truth, the real characteristics of the senses.

134
00:12:12,120 --> 00:12:15,202
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you get to see, oh yeah, truly, these are the five, six wild animals.

135
00:12:16,131 --> 00:12:16,693
[SPEAKER_01]: I have a choice.

136
00:12:17,515 --> 00:12:21,486
[SPEAKER_01]: I can allow them to run wild and then pay the price as a result of it.

137
00:12:23,482 --> 00:12:24,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, eh...

138
00:12:24,022 --> 00:12:39,967
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know if it's the same suit or the other suit, but it says, like, if I... if I let this cow go and eat wherever he wants, my ox, if I let it, then, you know, the owner will complain to the police, they'll come and they beat me up, they flog me, they fine me, because it was my bull, I'm responsible for it.

139
00:12:40,547 --> 00:12:41,347
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I don't...

140
00:12:41,367 --> 00:12:46,669
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't let it go and eat anywhere, wherever he wants, so I don't incur suffering on account of it.

141
00:12:47,971 --> 00:12:48,872
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't get out of control.

142
00:12:49,152 --> 00:12:59,060
[SPEAKER_01]: Or same like, like as I said, imagine like, you know, you're tied to five dogs, six dogs, and if you allow them, one of them to pull, they will gain the momentum, and then they'll be dragging you around.

143
00:12:59,781 --> 00:13:00,841
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be able to stand.

144
00:13:00,981 --> 00:13:05,125
[SPEAKER_01]: You... and then, if they choose to go through the rocky terrain and thorny terrain, you will.

145
00:13:05,305 --> 00:13:07,006
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't... you can't... oh, now I want to stop.

146
00:13:07,026 --> 00:13:07,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Now it's too late.

147
00:13:08,568 --> 00:13:11,730
[SPEAKER_01]: It will stop when it's running... when it runs... when it runs its course.

148
00:13:12,931 --> 00:13:15,073
[SPEAKER_01]: And by that time, you might be like beaten to death

149
00:13:16,425 --> 00:13:18,967
[SPEAKER_01]: As a result of those desires and wild animals.

150
00:13:20,469 --> 00:13:22,771
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, you're not trying to strangle the senses.

151
00:13:23,211 --> 00:13:34,822
[SPEAKER_01]: You're trying to maintain a better perspective in a sense of indirectly know that, oh, if I were to let this sense go out there now, there's a good likelihood it will see something, hear something, taste something.

152
00:13:35,342 --> 00:13:40,387
[SPEAKER_01]: They will then result in its increased excitement that then I will have to deal with.

153
00:13:42,747 --> 00:13:45,430
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's better for me to not allow it, then I don't have to deal with it.

154
00:13:45,490 --> 00:13:53,137
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I abide in peace here and now, as opposed to having to then spend all this effort to calm the sense down, calm the animal down.

155
00:13:53,658 --> 00:13:56,160
[SPEAKER_01]: No, just prevent it from being excited in the first place.

156
00:13:58,122 --> 00:14:02,967
[SPEAKER_01]: And the more you do that, the more you maintain that perspective, regardless of what sense is pulling you,

157
00:14:05,961 --> 00:14:09,442
[SPEAKER_01]: The clearer the nature of that relationship with the senses becomes.

158
00:14:09,542 --> 00:14:12,524
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, less and less identified with them you are.

159
00:14:13,224 --> 00:14:21,087
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why anāgāmīs and arahants in the suttas, when the Māra would try to tempt them through the senses, they would refer to the six senses, the body, the person.

160
00:14:21,107 --> 00:14:22,928
[SPEAKER_01]: They would say, who are you talking about?

161
00:14:23,008 --> 00:14:23,768
[SPEAKER_01]: None of this is mine.

162
00:14:23,788 --> 00:14:24,928
[SPEAKER_01]: This is just a creature there.

163
00:14:26,169 --> 00:14:28,350
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still the five, six functioning senses.

164
00:14:29,417 --> 00:14:37,499
[SPEAKER_01]: But in the same sense, you, from your point of view, would see a dog, a bird, a monkey, another person, and now there is this creature as well.

165
00:14:37,519 --> 00:14:52,684
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's a creature there, creature, creature, oh, and there's this creature that's always paired with me, because of which I used to assume it's mine, because there was no other creature as close as this creature, to my point of view, but now I understand that's not mine either.

166
00:14:53,044 --> 00:14:53,925
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you should regard it.

167
00:14:54,285 --> 00:14:58,506
[SPEAKER_01]: We usually regard our own creature, our own selves, as our own.

168
00:14:59,990 --> 00:15:04,554
[SPEAKER_01]: As the closest friend, so to speak, the most intimate thing, it's your own thoughts with yourself.

169
00:15:05,215 --> 00:15:08,417
[SPEAKER_01]: But actually, you should regard that also as not yours, as a stranger.

170
00:15:09,538 --> 00:15:11,880
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean you well, necessarily.

171
00:15:15,443 --> 00:15:18,686
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the creature is there, you're paired with it, the six senses.

172
00:15:19,066 --> 00:15:26,873
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no choice to be apart from the six senses, but you have a choice whether they get excited or not, and whether you're gonna have to be dealing with it or not.

173
00:15:28,263 --> 00:15:30,584
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why he guards the sense doors.

174
00:15:31,924 --> 00:15:34,745
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't chase the sense object or try to get rid of the sense objects.

175
00:15:35,586 --> 00:15:38,867
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't try to shut the eyes down or pull the eyes out or cut the ears off.

176
00:15:39,647 --> 00:15:40,968
[SPEAKER_01]: You guard the sense doors.

177
00:15:41,948 --> 00:15:52,692
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you know before the eye sees ears, hair and so on, is this leading towards lust or towards dispassion, towards anger or towards lack of anger and so on.

178
00:15:54,119 --> 00:15:56,861
[SPEAKER_01]: And if it still was lost, you guard the door, you don't let the animal...

179
00:15:56,901 --> 00:15:57,321
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same.

180
00:15:57,341 --> 00:15:58,422
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, imagine you're holding dog.

181
00:15:58,442 --> 00:15:58,962
[SPEAKER_01]: Dog is calm.

182
00:15:59,563 --> 00:16:00,784
[SPEAKER_01]: And you see there is a deer coming.

183
00:16:00,804 --> 00:16:02,144
[SPEAKER_01]: The dog still hasn't spotted it.

184
00:16:02,645 --> 00:16:03,145
[SPEAKER_01]: What would you do?

185
00:16:04,966 --> 00:16:07,728
[SPEAKER_01]: Kill the dog quickly, before it sees the deer, so you don't have to deal with it?

186
00:16:07,948 --> 00:16:10,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you kill the dog, you kill yourself, because you are the creature.

187
00:16:11,171 --> 00:16:12,912
[SPEAKER_01]: Shoot the deer before it comes?

188
00:16:12,932 --> 00:16:13,512
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, good luck.

189
00:16:14,153 --> 00:16:15,794
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what would you do in that instance?

190
00:16:16,897 --> 00:16:26,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Here you are, five dogs, both hands, leashes, they are calm, and there is a big, smelly deer coming the way, just behind the bend.

191
00:16:26,540 --> 00:16:28,000
[SPEAKER_01]: And you see it, the dogs don't see it.

192
00:16:28,300 --> 00:16:30,061
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, you turn around, so...

193
00:16:31,001 --> 00:16:31,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Turn around, yeah?

194
00:16:32,942 --> 00:16:38,203
[SPEAKER_02]: If you can't turn around... Well, you just get their attention, make them sit down.

195
00:16:39,243 --> 00:16:41,924
[SPEAKER_01]: Set them up, so they're not basically directly exposed to it.

196
00:16:42,364 --> 00:16:44,265
[SPEAKER_01]: If you can't do that either, what would you do then?

197
00:16:45,534 --> 00:16:45,934
[SPEAKER_02]: Tie them.

198
00:16:46,435 --> 00:16:47,535
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you hold them on the leashes.

199
00:16:47,735 --> 00:16:47,975
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

200
00:16:48,436 --> 00:16:48,676
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

201
00:16:49,897 --> 00:16:50,877
[SPEAKER_01]: You hold them down.

202
00:16:50,897 --> 00:16:51,838
[SPEAKER_01]: You dig your heels in.

203
00:16:52,238 --> 00:16:52,438
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

204
00:16:53,198 --> 00:16:56,540
[SPEAKER_01]: Twist the leashes twice around your hands and pull down and hold.

205
00:16:57,661 --> 00:17:00,563
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, any of those things, the result is the same.

206
00:17:00,983 --> 00:17:02,844
[SPEAKER_01]: They must not chase the deer.

207
00:17:03,324 --> 00:17:11,109
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, for as... until you're beyond sensuality and so on, the animals will get excited.

208
00:17:11,169 --> 00:17:13,030
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't want to be contributing towards that.

209
00:17:13,070 --> 00:17:13,950
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's that whole, like,

210
00:17:16,807 --> 00:17:26,189
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't remember the exact sutra, but when the Buddha says, you know, or you think the opposite, or you think something else, but if you can't do any of that, you crush your mind with the mind.

211
00:17:26,629 --> 00:17:29,470
[SPEAKER_01]: Like a drunk man would pin down the weak man until he calms down.

212
00:17:30,950 --> 00:17:42,633
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if the dogs are now, you couldn't prevent the excitement, as it will inevitably happen at some point, because you didn't foresee the deer soon enough, you were careless, or whatever.

213
00:17:43,658 --> 00:17:44,479
[SPEAKER_01]: Now they're excited.

214
00:17:44,559 --> 00:17:52,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Still, it's better to endure and not give in to the pull, than to let it and then become the dogs and get identified with the creature.

215
00:17:54,354 --> 00:17:58,160
[SPEAKER_01]: And the more you practice that, then the clearer also the danger of allowing it becomes.

216
00:17:59,643 --> 00:18:07,727
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when all you're used to is being dragged around and surfing on the leashes of various wild animals, you don't see the danger, because you're just used to the constant movement.

217
00:18:07,767 --> 00:18:11,008
[SPEAKER_02]: And also, that's what you're training them to become.

218
00:18:11,688 --> 00:18:12,049
[SPEAKER_02]: For sure.

219
00:18:12,189 --> 00:18:13,729
[SPEAKER_01]: You're training them to be wild.

220
00:18:14,209 --> 00:18:15,690
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly how the passion works.

221
00:18:15,770 --> 00:18:21,113
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the Buddha means by diversification, proliferation or multiplying.

222
00:18:21,493 --> 00:18:23,734
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you choose the singleness of no,

223
00:18:24,494 --> 00:18:32,757
[SPEAKER_01]: As opposed to diversity, of spread in every direction, and then one deer, two deer, six deers, fox, rabbit, squirrel...

224
00:18:35,556 --> 00:18:44,341
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's what... people think singleness of mind is with... where you kind of... at the end of this proliferation, you find a point that all proliferation ceases.

225
00:18:44,682 --> 00:18:44,822
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

226
00:18:45,442 --> 00:18:51,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Singleness is actually when you undo all the proliferation and find a point before things proliferate.

227
00:18:51,926 --> 00:18:54,208
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you choose to stay on that level and protect it.

228
00:18:54,348 --> 00:18:55,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Guarding the door.

229
00:18:55,689 --> 00:18:56,369
[SPEAKER_01]: One entrance.

230
00:18:56,969 --> 00:18:57,830
[SPEAKER_01]: One pointedness.

231
00:18:59,617 --> 00:19:02,739
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not one-pointed because you stare at the deer, you don't see anything else.

232
00:19:02,780 --> 00:19:11,526
[SPEAKER_01]: You're one-pointed because your point is none of the situation will change, doesn't matter whether deer or squirrel, whatever comes in here, dog will not run.

233
00:19:12,747 --> 00:19:16,310
[SPEAKER_01]: And if they're used to running all the time, they're going to be pulling a lot in the beginning.

234
00:19:17,211 --> 00:19:19,973
[SPEAKER_01]: Untrained animal.

235
00:19:19,993 --> 00:19:20,554
[SPEAKER_01]: So, same with you.

236
00:19:20,574 --> 00:19:28,300
[SPEAKER_01]: If you haven't been really developing and clarifying perspective in regard to the sensory strength, if you've just been doing it blindly,

237
00:19:30,642 --> 00:19:31,462
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still gonna hurt.

238
00:19:32,263 --> 00:19:42,366
[SPEAKER_01]: But once the perspective is there, the singleness of mind, that unity, so to speak, of non-diversified in different direction and so on, remains, even while the sense is excited.

239
00:19:43,906 --> 00:19:47,327
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is no identification with it, because there is no perspective loss.

240
00:19:50,168 --> 00:19:57,691
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you and the creature, your point of view in regard to the creature, and the thoughts of the creature, and the plans of the creature, and the sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches of the creature,

241
00:19:58,550 --> 00:19:59,691
[SPEAKER_01]: None that's yours, in that sense.

242
00:19:59,731 --> 00:20:02,153
[SPEAKER_01]: But not to say, oh, no, no, not mine, not mine, to dismiss it.

243
00:20:02,293 --> 00:20:05,356
[SPEAKER_01]: But not mine, because it's there and I have perspective in regard to it.

244
00:20:06,036 --> 00:20:06,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Not my dogs.

245
00:20:07,438 --> 00:20:07,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

246
00:20:07,878 --> 00:20:08,579
[SPEAKER_01]: Not my dogs.

247
00:20:08,619 --> 00:20:10,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's often the response.

248
00:20:11,801 --> 00:20:12,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Or denial.

249
00:20:13,883 --> 00:20:14,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Or acceptance.

250
00:20:14,844 --> 00:20:16,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, well, that's what the dogs do.

251
00:20:16,565 --> 00:20:18,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yes, but you don't have to suffer on account of it.

252
00:20:19,388 --> 00:20:22,070
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the trained dog wouldn't do that.

253
00:20:22,090 --> 00:20:24,372
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't say that's what all the dogs do.

254
00:20:25,783 --> 00:20:33,571
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to qualify that statement and say that's what the untrained dogs do, which now highlights you have a choice to train or to untrain.

255
00:20:34,792 --> 00:20:35,593
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what we say.

256
00:20:36,313 --> 00:20:41,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Untrained eye, untrained ear, untrained senses, untrained mind in regard to the senses, that's what it would do.

257
00:20:42,219 --> 00:20:43,120
[SPEAKER_01]: So you train it.

258
00:20:44,161 --> 00:20:46,884
[SPEAKER_01]: Take responsibility for the lack of training and train it.

259
00:20:49,094 --> 00:20:53,277
[SPEAKER_01]: And the clearer your perspective is, the more it becomes obvious it's an indirect training.

260
00:20:53,417 --> 00:21:01,602
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's kind of... Sensory training is actually effortless, if done rightly as well, because it's on the basis of mindfulness, which you can't have too much of, and it is effortless, if done rightly.

261
00:21:02,963 --> 00:21:07,006
[SPEAKER_01]: All you need to do, and even if the excitement happens, it's not your excitement.

262
00:21:08,527 --> 00:21:08,927
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know.

263
00:21:09,067 --> 00:21:10,768
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter how much this dog is excited.

264
00:21:10,928 --> 00:21:11,689
[SPEAKER_01]: He's not going anywhere.

265
00:21:13,911 --> 00:21:14,611
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then it comes down.

266
00:21:14,872 --> 00:21:23,957
[SPEAKER_01]: You might have to hear it, listen to it yapping, but that's the disturbance that you have to accept on the level that arahant accepts the disturbance left on account of this body until the aggregates break apart.

267
00:21:25,058 --> 00:21:30,181
[SPEAKER_01]: But, for sure, his animals will not be able to pull him anymore, ever again, at all.

268
00:21:30,321 --> 00:21:30,521
[SPEAKER_01]: Done.

269
00:21:31,682 --> 00:21:33,283
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he senses that in his control.

270
00:21:38,025 --> 00:21:40,146
[SPEAKER_02]: The spirit has been broken in a way.

271
00:21:40,206 --> 00:21:40,486
[SPEAKER_01]: Tamed.

272
00:21:40,926 --> 00:21:41,526
[SPEAKER_01]: They've been tamed.

273
00:21:41,967 --> 00:21:44,327
[SPEAKER_01]: They've been well tamed and trained and obedient.

274
00:21:45,168 --> 00:21:46,988
[SPEAKER_01]: So now, both are dwelling at ease.

275
00:21:47,989 --> 00:21:49,989
[SPEAKER_01]: Because senses are happier as well.

276
00:21:50,569 --> 00:21:56,351
[SPEAKER_01]: That's then the pleasure of jhāna and, you know, touching the pleasure with the body, if the meditation is done correctly and so on.

277
00:21:57,112 --> 00:22:03,874
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more pleasant to be peaceful, even for the wild animals.

278
00:22:04,774 --> 00:22:05,975
[SPEAKER_01]: But they can't help themselves.

279
00:22:06,435 --> 00:22:06,895
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can.

280
00:22:08,167 --> 00:22:09,968
[SPEAKER_01]: And by doing so, you're helping yourself as well.

281
00:22:10,729 --> 00:22:14,671
[SPEAKER_01]: Training the creature that is you.

282
00:22:14,972 --> 00:22:26,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's exactly like training dogs.

283
00:22:26,800 --> 00:22:26,980
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

284
00:22:27,080 --> 00:22:28,821
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's often the similitude.

285
00:22:28,901 --> 00:22:33,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Taming an elephant, taming an ox, that the Buddha used in the suttas.

286
00:22:33,144 --> 00:22:34,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Elephant, yeah.

287
00:22:36,899 --> 00:22:42,343
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I said, the difference is whether you're kind of projecting it externally or... No, you have to see it yourself.

288
00:22:42,403 --> 00:22:45,206
[SPEAKER_01]: Your five, six senses as five, six dogs.

289
00:22:46,887 --> 00:22:51,991
[SPEAKER_01]: And they all have their different little traits and little characteristics and little kind of preferences.

290
00:22:54,473 --> 00:22:55,414
[SPEAKER_01]: But you are the one.

291
00:22:55,834 --> 00:22:59,597
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, you don't train them by constantly choking them and holding on to them.

292
00:23:00,118 --> 00:23:02,400
[SPEAKER_01]: You train them by seeing more than what they see.

293
00:23:03,264 --> 00:23:06,825
[SPEAKER_01]: By maintaining the background to their own actions that they're not aware of.

294
00:23:07,525 --> 00:23:10,606
[SPEAKER_01]: Look, he's running like a maniac now, and there are wild boars there.

295
00:23:10,946 --> 00:23:13,406
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't see it, and when he does, it will be too late.

296
00:23:14,487 --> 00:23:16,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, imagine you're tied to that dog.

297
00:23:16,427 --> 00:23:20,068
[SPEAKER_01]: It's dragging you where the wild boars are, angry with little babies.

298
00:23:21,648 --> 00:23:25,349
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, so now my life is at stake, so I better do something about it.

299
00:23:26,750 --> 00:23:28,490
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just... Oh, it's not my problem.

300
00:23:30,700 --> 00:23:37,866
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in order to stop identifying with the six animals that your creature is, you have to take them up personally first.

301
00:23:38,666 --> 00:23:40,908
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to actually see that you are at stake here.

302
00:23:42,329 --> 00:23:47,333
[SPEAKER_01]: If you let your mind run, eyes, ears, nose and so on, you will pay the price, not them.

303
00:23:48,934 --> 00:23:52,237
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I better take it this personally.

304
00:23:58,527 --> 00:24:08,275
[SPEAKER_01]: You end up taking it personally, you tame them eventually, and then you cannot identify with them, because you can't lose perspective now.

305
00:24:12,339 --> 00:24:12,919
[SPEAKER_02]: And patience.

306
00:24:13,520 --> 00:24:14,420
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's the key.

307
00:24:14,440 --> 00:24:17,063
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's any... even the basic dog training.

308
00:24:17,103 --> 00:24:19,024
[SPEAKER_01]: The first thing they say, you must be patient.

309
00:24:20,586 --> 00:24:25,510
[SPEAKER_01]: And imagine how much more, when it comes to training yourself as a creature of the six.

310
00:24:29,465 --> 00:24:30,247
[SPEAKER_02]: The wild man.

311
00:24:30,427 --> 00:24:31,370
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, the wild man.

312
00:24:31,690 --> 00:24:33,254
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, everybody's a wild creature, yeah.

